#**********************************************************************#
#  ME 697 class project with a reactive Material X: ThermalCascade     #
#                                                                      #
#  The class problem (me697_double_shock_2p00.deck), with Material X   #
#  burning by ThermalCascade, the second of bitwhiz's burn models      #
#  (doc/design/burn_models.md): proposed by ChatGPT 4o-mini in         #
#  SAND2025-09228, repaired, and gated by the lead shock:              #
#                                                                      #
#      dlam/dt = HT (1 - lam) max(T - TC, 0)^GT (1 + P_s/PR)^NS        #
#                                                                      #
#  once the cell's lead shock is marked (its pressure, P_s, frozen     #
#  there; before it, nothing burns). T is the closure's temperature,   #
#  solid and products alike, so burning heats the cell and speeds the  #
#  burn: the cascade.                                                  #
#                                                                      #
#      | Ta 3.0 | Al 4.0 |=> Material X 10.0 | PMMA 10.0 |  PDV ->     #
#     x=-7.0           x=0 (impact plane)    x=10.0      x=20.0        #
#                                                                      #
#  HT, TC, GT and NS here are an example, not a calibration (that is   #
#  the design note's step 10): chosen so that at 2.00 km/s the shock   #
#  grows to a detonation about 3.5 mm into Material X, as HVRB's does  #
#  (me697_hvrb_2p00.deck). The shocked solid is near 740 K at the      #
#  impact's 11 GPa, above TC = 600 K, so it starts to burn behind the  #
#  shock; the products are the HVRB deck's ideal gas. The burn's keys, #
#  and what each does: input/README.md, Material block; every other    #
#  key is the class deck's (its key reference, at its end). Units: mm, #
#  us, g/cc, km/s, GPa, K.                                             #
#**********************************************************************#
Begin bitwhiz
 deck_version = 1
 define u_fly = 2.00            # km/s, flyer velocity

 Begin Run
  plots_only = no    # yes or no
  title = tcas_c0p3
  t_end       = 3.0             # us: the detonation reaches the PDV
                                # interface near 1.5 us at 2.00 km/s
  num_threads = 1
 End Run

 Begin Output                   # nothing listed here = nothing written
  
                                # pixel row of the t-x diagrams
  
  tracer_dt = 0.0           # us between tracer rows (the
                                # reference's spacing); 0 = every cycle
  colormap    = viridis         # for bitmap pictures

  tracer = pdv     MatX:PMMA    # the PDV signal
  tracer = impact  Al:MatX      # impact plane
  tracer = ta_al   Ta:Al        # where the second shock is born
  tracer = mid_x   MatX@0.5     # mid-sample, past the run distance
 End Output

 Begin Solver
  method = RS                   # the Riemann solver: a shock 1-2 cells
  order  = 2                    # sharp, and a smooth burn behind it
                                # (second order, MUSCL-Hancock)
  BL     = 0.1                  # method = AV's linear and quadratic
  BQ     = 2.0                  # viscosity coefficients (the equation
                                # sheet's defaults); RS ignores them
  CFL = 0.3
 End Solver

 # Mie-Gruneisen on the principal Hugoniot (rev-0, Eqs. 4 and 6):
 #   Us = C0 + S1*up + (S2/C0)*up^2,   rho*Gamma = R0*G0
 Begin Material Ta              # tantalum, backing plate
  R0 = 16.65                    # g/cm^3, initial density
  C0 = 3.40                     # km/s, Hugoniot intercept
  S1 = 1.20                     # -,    Us-up slope
  S2 = 0.0                      # -,    Us-up curvature (default 0)
  G0 = 1.60                     # -,    Gruneisen coefficient
  spall_strength = 5.0          # GPa, dynamic. Tantalum is strong in
                                # tension (spall strengths of several
                                # GPa are reported); at 5.0 the Ta here
                                # holds its ~2 GPa tension, as in the
                                # reference data. Other materials take
                                # the default, 0.01 GPa (input/README.md)
 End Material

 Begin Material Al              # aluminum, flyer face
  R0 = 2.78
  C0 = 5.30
  S1 = 1.34
  S2 = 0.0
  G0 = 2.00
 End Material

 Begin Material MatX            # notional HE surrogate, reactive
  eos  = mie_gruneisen_t        # the unreacted solid, plus T: the burn's
  R0   = 1.60                   # closure mixes it with the products at
  C0   = 2.50                   # one density and one temperature
  S1   = 1.80
  S2   = -0.10
  G0   = 1.20
  CV   = 1.1633418              # J/(g K), both phases
  T0   = 298.0                  # K

  burn = thermalcascade         # ThermalCascade, gated by the lead shock
  HT   = 3.0e-5                 # 1/(us K^GT), rate coefficient
  TC   = 600.0                  # K, critical temperature: no burn at or
                                # below it
  GT   = 1.5                    # temperature exponent
  NS   = 2.0                    # exponent on (1 + P_s/PR)
  PR   = 7.0                    # GPa, reference pressure

  products = ideal_gas          # P = (GAMMA - 1) rho CV T, as the HVRB
                                # deck's
  GAMMA    = 2.60
  Q        = 4.8828             # kJ/g (= MJ/kg), heat of reaction
 End Material

 Begin Material PMMA            # window; Lacina et al. (2018) linear fit
  R0 = 1.187
  C0 = 2.55
  S1 = 1.54
  S2 = 0.0
  G0 = 0.90                     # estimated from Plexiglass (rev-0 Table 1)
 End Material

 Begin Stack
  resolution = 35               # zones/mm: 105 + 140 + 350 + 350 = 945
  origin     = Al:MatX          # x = 0 at the impact plane

  Begin Layer
   material = Ta
   width    = 3.0               # mm
   xvel     = u_fly             # km/s, + is to the right
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   material = Al
   width    = 4.0
   xvel     = u_fly
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   material = MatX
   width    = 10.0
  End Layer

  Begin Layer
   material = PMMA
   width    = 10.0
  End Layer
 End Stack
End bitwhiz

Notes (ignored by the parser):
- Velocity sweep: change "define u_fly" only. This model's threshold is
  in T, so it is sharper than HVRB's (measured at this deck's 35
  zones/mm, RS order 2): 2.00 km/s detonates about 3.5 mm in, 1.75 about
  5.5 mm; at 1.50 the shocked solid is just over TC (about 710 K at 5
  mm), and a slow burn behind the shock (lam about 0.03 at 5 mm by 3 us)
  never detonates within 10 mm; at 1.25 only the cell at the impact face
  burns (the impact's start-up heats it far over TC), and at 1.00 nothing
  burns. (HVRB, this deck's twin: 3.5, 5.0 and 7.5 mm at 2.00, 1.75 and
  1.50.) Calibration (step 10) will choose HT, TC, GT and NS to follow
  HVRB's Pop plot.
- Each tracer's XRN column (the tracer file) is its cell's extent of
  reaction, lam, 0 to 1; TK its temperature (in Material X). TK rises as
  lam does: that is the cascade.
- The lead shock: a cell burns only after its lead shock is marked (SURF's
  detector, Menikoff and Shaw 2010; doc/design/burn_models.md, step 4).
  A smooth ramp is marked too, at its foot, with P_s near 0: then the
  rate is HT (T - TC)^GT, and only T decides (the design note, step 5).
- Method RS at order 2 (the Solver block), as of 1.4.0: a sharp shock
  and a smooth burn behind it. Try method = AV to compare: its shock is
  smeared over a few cells, and at 1.50 km/s its shocked solid stays
  nearer TC (about 590 K at 5 mm), so almost nothing burns. Or GT = 0:
  above TC the rate is then HT (1 + P_s/PR)^NS, a clock (input/README.md).
- "Cells burnt out" on the screen counts lam = 1 exactly, which this
  model's exact step reaches only when 1 - lam rounds away: a detonated
  cell is at lam = 1 to round-off whether or not it is counted.
